# 2026-08-15 round 180. The element is 16 bits AND its low byte is inert, which
# only a float can do. Feed a real fp16.
#
# ROUND 179 CONFIRMED THE PAIRING and the layout for k under 16 is now complete.
# With each value in the high byte of a 16 bit slot, --kpair reads
#
#   byte 0 low -> k 0    byte 0 high -> k 1    byte 1 low -> k 2
#   byte 7 high -> k 15  byte 8 low  -> k 16 on channel 1
#   byte 512 low -> k 0 on channel 32
#
# which is exactly k = 2b + h, where the 8 bit packing read 2k + 1. So the
# element is 16 bits wide, and the intra row order is the interleaved one, order
# 0, not the halved one. Together with the row measured in round 172:
#
#   channel n is read from byte (n / 32) * 512 + (n % 32) * 8,
#   byte b nibble h is k = 2b + h, with an element two bytes wide
#
# and every part of that has now been measured and then confirmed by a second,
# different probe. It also retires "the fetch is a quarter of the buffer", which
# this project carried from round 172: it was never short, it is a full fetch of
# half as many elements, each twice as wide.
#
# THE VALUES ARE STILL AT THE RAILS, and the reason is in the same log. In the
# sweep, a one hot of 100 placed in the LOW byte of an element never lit a
# channel. A 16 bit INTEGER cannot do that: 100 would be about 100 output counts,
# which is plainly visible. A FLOAT must do it, because a value whose exponent
# byte is zero is a subnormal near zero.
#
# So the activation is an fp16 here, which is the only way the vendor runs int4,
# as w4a16. Writing an int8 byte into the high half of an fp16 makes a float with
# that byte as its sign and exponent: garbage of a wildly varying magnitude, and
# the impulse duly rails.
#
# I could not predict the observed values from an fp16 reinterpretation either,
# and that is recorded rather than smoothed over. Every integer domain model of
# the arithmetic fails on them, which is what a float datapath would look like,
# but "the element is 16 bits" is measured and "the element is a float" is an
# inference from one negative.
#
# ROUND 180's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   ENTRY 3, --f16. A REAL fp16 activation, small integers a half holds exactly
#   so the packing adds no error of its own, with one live nibble per channel at
#   k = c mod 16.
#
#     the output tracks the activation
#               int4 computes, w4a16 is the configuration it needs, and what is
#               left is only where k = 16 and above go.
#     the output is still at the rails
#               the element being 16 bits stands, since that was measured twice,
#               and its being a FLOAT is what comes down. The next suspect is
#               then the weight nibble's own format rather than the activation.
#     the sentinel survives
#               nothing was written at all, and an fp16 activation needs
#               register changes this round did not make.
#
#   ENTRY 4 keeps the integer impulse as the baseline being explained, and
#   ENTRY 5 is int8, which must be untouched.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What earlier rounds settled, kept because the rules below were written against
# it.
#
# 2026-08-15 round 179. int4 CONSUMES THE ACTIVATION AS 16 BITS. Measured, and
# this round packs the input that way.
#
# ROUND 178's RESULT, and it is the cleanest reading in this whole stretch. One
# live nibble in the whole buffer, a one hot input, sweeping k:
#
#   byte 0 low  -> k 1     byte 0 high -> k 3
#   byte 1 low  -> k 5     byte 1 high -> k 7
#   byte 2 low  -> k 9     byte 2 high -> k 11
#   byte 3 low  -> k 13    byte 3 high -> k 15
#   byte 4 low  -> k 17    byte 4 high -> k 19
#   byte 7 low  -> k 29    byte 7 high -> k 31
#
# EVERY nibble paired with EXACTLY ONE k, 1 of 64 on all twelve offsets. So the
# layout is a permutation, nothing is broadcast, and the "a nibble reaches more
# than one input" reading that has stood since round 171 is dead.
#
# The pairing is k = 4b + 2h + 1, and against what the packer intends, k = 2b + h,
# that is exactly
#
#     k_hardware = 2 * k_ours + 1
#
# with no exception in twelve. That is what a 16 BIT ELEMENT does to an 8 bit
# buffer: the hardware's element k is our bytes 2k and 2k+1, and it pairs with
# the high one. int4 weights consume the activation as 16 bits whatever the
# stream asks for, which is also the only way the vendor ever runs int4, as
# w4a16.
#
# It settles the rest at a stroke. A row of 8 bytes is 16 nibbles and therefore
# 16 of the HARDWARE's elements, not 16 of ours, so channel 0 takes our k = 1 to
# 31 odd and channel 1 takes our k = 33 to 63 odd. Byte 8 measured exactly that.
# And "the fetch is a quarter of the buffer" was never a short fetch: it is a
# full fetch of half as many elements, each twice as wide.
#
# ROUND 179's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run. The input
# packer now writes each value into the HIGH byte of a 16 bit slot, and the atom,
# the surface stride and the packing all come from one place,
# charsiu_effective_adtype, so they cannot describe different buffers. surf for
# K = 64 int4 goes from 1 to 2, which is the 16 bit atom of 8 rather than the 8
# bit atom of 16.
#
#   ENTRY 3, the int4 impulse.
#
#     byte exact against a reference that is not flat
#               int4 computes, and the last thing between charsiu and a 2x on
#               every token is where k = 16 and above go.
#     the right channels light with wrong values
#               the pairing is right and something in the arithmetic is not,
#               which is a much smaller place to look than a layout.
#     unchanged from round 177
#               the 16 bit reading is wrong despite fitting twelve of twelve,
#               and the control below says whether the change even took.
#
#   ENTRY 4 IS THE CONTROL. CHARSIU_A8_STRIDE1 restores the 8 bit packing, so
#   round 177's saturated rails must come back. A fix whose control does not
#   bring the fault back has not been shown to be the fix.
#
#   ENTRY 5 is int8, which must be untouched: charsiu_effective_adtype returns
#   the asked for type for every precision except int4.
#
