#   npu[0..15]  128 128 127 127 128 127 127 128 127 127 128 128 127 128 144 127
#
# 128 is a requant clamped to zero and 127 is saturation. That is not a
# permutation of the reference, it is a scale that has blown up.
#
# AND THE SAME SCALE WORKS IN THE MAP PROBE. --map runs weight_scale 1.7857 with
# a single live nibble and produces clean small values, and on int8 it produces
# v = k + 1 exactly. The only difference is how many live nibbles exist: one in
# the whole buffer for the map, sixty four for the impulse, one per channel. If
# the layout were right each channel would still see exactly one, so saturation
# says channels are seeing each other's.
#
# That lines up with the one fact this project has left standing as unexplained
# since round 171: the map's k column is unusable because A NIBBLE REACHES MORE
# THAN ONE INPUT. Cross talk between channels and a nibble touching several
# inputs are the same statement.
#
# It has never been measured because every probe so far is sparse in one operand
# and DENSE in the other, so every output is a sum over whatever the nibble
# touches and no single k can be named.
#
# ROUND 178's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   ENTRY 3, --kpair. ONE live nibble in the whole buffer, a ONE HOT input at a
#   single k, sweeping k across all of K. The output is nonzero exactly when that
#   nibble is paired with that k, so the set of inputs a nibble touches is read
#   directly with no sum to unpick. Eight byte offsets, both nibbles.
#
#     exactly one k lights per nibble
#               the pairing is one to one, the layout is a permutation after
#               all, and the k column can finally be built from these hits
#               rather than from a value.
#     several k light per nibble
#               a nibble is broadcast across inputs and the count says by how
#               much. That would explain the map's unusable k column AND round
#               177's saturation with one mechanism, and it would mean the
#               weight buffer is not a plain tiling at this precision.
#     no k lights at an offset the map says is live
#               the one hot input is below what the output stage can show, and
#               the amplitude has to come up before anything else is read.
#
#   ENTRY 4 keeps round 177's impulse as the baseline, so the saturation this
#   round is explaining stays on the same page as the explanation.
#
#   ENTRY 5 IS THE CONTROL and int8 must still be byte exact.
