# What earlier rounds settled, kept because the rules below were written against
# it.
#
# 2026-08-15 round 175. Round 174's int8 map was saturated by my own probe, so
# the comparison it was for did not happen.
#
# WHAT ROUND 174 SHOWED. Every int8 probe lit all 64 channels at 127, INCLUDING
# the one with an all zero weight buffer. That is not the hardware, it is the
# probe: this tool set weight_zero_point to 0 for both precisions, which is right
# for a nibble and wrong for a byte. An int8 weight is stored biased by 0x80, so
# its zero point is 0x80 and its B record should be 0; with 0 the B comes out
# 0x80, and 0x80 * 2080 * mult saturates every channel whatever the buffer holds.
#
# The rule written before that run said an int8 map with dead regions would mean
# the probe is measuring the wrong thing in both precisions. It saturated rather
# than went dead, but the same conclusion applies to the int8 half: no comparison
# was made. The int4 half is unaffected, since a zero point of 0 IS right for a
# nibble, so rounds 172 and 173 stand.
#
# The probe now takes its zero point from the precision, and PRINTS both in its
# banner, because nothing in round 174's output said which was in use.
#
# WHAT STANDS ABOUT int4, all of it measured:
#
#   the row is 8 bytes, 16 nibbles, at K = 32, 64 and 128 and at N = 32 and 64
#   the fetch is N * 8 bytes and doubling K fetches nothing more
#   64 of 256 byte offsets light, a QUARTER of the buffer
#   every lit probe lights exactly one channel, 512 probes, no exceptions
#   channels 0 to 31 read from bytes 0 to 255, channels 32 to 63 from 512 to
#   767, and 256 to 511 is dead: stride 512 against a use of 256
#
# 16 nibbles is exactly the int8 activation feature atom these probes run with.
#
# ROUND 175's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run. It is
# round 174's, with a probe that can answer it.
#
#   ENTRY 3 maps int8 and int4 at the same shape, same stride, same probe, with
#   the weight zero point now correct for each.
#
#     int8's map covers its whole buffer and int4's a quarter
#               the fetch is short only for int4, and the difference between the
#               two maps names what does it.
#     int8's map is also short
#               the shortfall is not about precision at all, and the probe's own
#               geometry is the next suspect rather than the weight path.
#     int8 saturates again
#               the zero point was not the only thing wrong with it, and the
#               int8 arm of this comparison should be abandoned in favour of
#               reading Mesa's weight buffer instead, which is known correct.
#
