# 2026-08-15 round 171. ROUNDS 167 AND 168 ARE WITHDRAWN. A probe that fills the
# whole buffer cannot see a permutation, and both of theirs did.
#
# ROUND 170b's RESULT, once the scale discriminated. The int4 impulse puts one
# live weight in every output channel, so every channel should be nonzero, and
# the reference is. The hardware lit 14 of 64, at 0, 4, 8, 16, 20, 24, 28, 34,
# 38, 42, 50, 54, 58 and 62. The live nibbles are landing in the wrong places, so
# the layout is wrong.
#
# WHY THE EARLIER ROUNDS COULD NOT HAVE SEEN IT, which is the part worth keeping.
# Round 167 filled EVERY low nibble, then EVERY high nibble. Round 168 filled
# every low nibble and walked an input impulse. A full fill is invariant under
# any layout that is a bijection: "all low live" gives "all channels equal"
# whatever the placement, so the two probes agreed with each other and with
# nothing else. They were measuring that the layout is a permutation, which was
# never in doubt.
#
# A layout can only be read with a SPARSE probe. The int8 one was read with
# position encoded models and this is the same idea.
#
# THE CONTROL EARNED ITS PLACE. Round 169's configuration under
# CHARSIU_W4_SMALLSCALE came back 54 of 64 exact with "reference spread: 1
# distinct, 0..0 TOO FLAT TO JUDGE A MATCH" beside it: the number and the reason
# it means nothing, on the same line.
#
# AND ONE OF OUR OWN NUMBERS IS WEAKER THAN IT LOOKED. The int8 512 to 1024
# matmul reads 1023 of 1024 byte exact against a reference with 7 distinct
# values, so most of those matches are common values rather than computed ones.
# The 64 wide result, 64 distinct and 64 of 64, is the strong one. The charsiu
# README said 1023 of 1024 without that qualification and now says it.
#
# ROUND 171's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   ENTRY 3, --map. ONE live nibble in the whole buffer at a time, sixteen byte
#   offsets and both nibbles of each. The scales are set so the answer needs no
#   arithmetic: the output CHANNEL that lights is n and its VALUE is k + 1.
#
#     each probe lights exactly one channel
#               the map is read directly and the packer follows from it.
#     a probe lights several channels
#               one nibble reaches more than one output, which is not a
#               permutation and would mean the weight buffer is being broadcast
#               rather than tiled.
#     NOTHING LIT on probes that should light
#               a single live nibble is below what the output stage can show,
#               and the probe needs a larger amplitude before anything else.
#
#   The layout withdrawn this round predicts byte b low to be n = b / 32 and
#   k = b % 32, and byte b high the same n with k + 32. The log will say directly
#   whether that is what happens, which is worth having on the record even though
#   round 170b already ruled it out by another route.
