# 2026-08-15 round 170b. The round 170 image did not carry round 170's binary.
# int4 is not confirmed, including its layout.
#
# WHAT WENT WRONG WITH 170, because it is a process fault and not a hardware one.
# Its script ran and its two int4 entries were byte identical to round 169's, 54
# of 64 both at the new scale and under the control that was supposed to restore
# the old one, and no line printed the reference spread the tool was supposed to
# print. The binary was stale: the edit that raised the int4 weight scale and the
# edit that added the spread line were in the same scripted change, that change
# asserted on a third anchor that had gone stale, and the assert aborted the
# write before ANY of the three landed. Only the third was re-applied, because
# the abort was read as "the first two went in".
#
# The assert did its job. The reading of it did not. From here the check is
# `strings` on the BINARY for the new text, not a look at the source, which is
# the same rule this project already had about timestamps.
#
# Round 170b is round 170 with the binary that round 170 was supposed to have.
#
# WHAT ROUND 169 ACTUALLY SAID. The control passed, int8 unchanged at 1023 of
# 1024 and 64 of 64 with NO_LIFT still 0 of 64, so the packer, both group helpers
# and the B record all changed without moving it. Then:
#
#   int4 bias ramp   64 of 64 BYTE EXACT
#   int4 impulse     54 of 64 exact, the other 10 off by 1 or 2
#   int4 dense 64    6 of 64
#   int4 dense 512   95 of 1024
#
# The first reading of that was "the layout is right and the accumulation is
# not". THAT READING IS WITHDRAWN. The weight scale was left at int8's while a
# nibble spans -7 to 7 instead of -128 to 127, so the whole reference vector fell
# into -1 to +1. Of the impulse's 54 matches, essentially all are channels where
# the reference is zero and the hardware is zero, which is not a match at all;
# and every channel that carried any signal was wrong by about half of the range
# it could hold. An error of one output count is 1250 accumulator units here, and
# a single live tap can only produce 120 of them.
#
# So the only clean result in round 169 is the bias ramp, and the bias ramp does
# not touch a weight: it holds the MAC at zero and measures the output stage
# alone. int4's registers, requant, lift and offset are right. Its LAYOUT IS NOT
# CONFIRMED, and neither is anything else about its weight path.
#
# A matching channel is not a computed one. This project has a rule about that,
# from the Mesa side, and round 169 broke it.
#
# ROUND 170's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   The int4 weight scale is 18 times int8's now, which puts the accumulator
#   range where int8's is, so a match means something and one count means one
#   count. Every result line also prints the REFERENCE's own spread beside the
#   match count, and says TOO FLAT TO JUDGE A MATCH when it has fewer than eight
#   distinct values, so round 169's mistake cannot be made quietly again. The
#   impulse uses a real nibble of 7 rather than an int8 constant masked down to 4.
#
#   ENTRY 3, the int4 impulse. One live nibble per channel, and the output IS the
#   layout read directly.
#
#     byte exact, with a reference that is not flat
#               the layout decoded in rounds 167 and 168 is confirmed against
#               real values, and the dense case is next.
#     structurally right, wrong values
#               layout right, arithmetic wrong, and B or the nibble sign
#               convention is where to look.
#     the live channels are in the wrong places
#               the layout is wrong and the pattern rounds were reading
#               something else. Position encoded weights, not live or dead ones,
#               are then the next probe.
#
#   ENTRY 4 is the dense matmul, which only means anything if the impulse passes.
#
#   ENTRY 5 IS THE CONTROL, and it is round 169's own configuration under
#   CHARSIU_W4_SMALLSCALE. It must reproduce round 169's flat reference and its
#   54 of 64, or the scale is not what made this round different.
