# What earlier rounds settled, kept because the rules below were written against
# it.
#
# 2026-08-15 round 169. THE int4 LAYOUT IS DECODED. This is the first round that
# asks it for a NUMBER rather than a pattern.
#
# ROUND 168's RESULT, and it is unambiguous. With the low nibbles live, an input
# impulse walking k moved the output for k = 0 through 31 and did nothing at all
# for k = 32, 33, 62 and 63:
#
#   k     0   1   2   3   15  16  30  31  32  33  62  63
#   delta +3  +5  +3  +3  +3  +3  +3  +3  +0  +0  +0  +0
#
# So it is not k with k+1 interleaved, which would have moved on even k only. A
# byte holds k and k+32, the two halves of a group of 64. With round 167's result
# that both nibbles are the same output channel, the layout is
#
#   [n/32][k/64][n%32][k%64],  byte = k % 32,  nibble = (k % 64) / 32
#
# which is the int8 tile with its K group doubled and folded, so a row is 32
# bytes at either precision and the tile shape does not change with it. The
# RK3588 table is wrong here in both places: the N group is 32 and not 64, and
# the K group is 64 and not 32.
#
# The packer is written and unit checked on the host, 4096 single weight
# placements each landing at the right byte and nibble and nowhere else, which is
# `make test` in the charsiu tree. An off by one in a packer is invisible in an
# output vector and costs a whole board round.
#
# AND THE 105 IS EXPLAINED. Round 167's every-nibble-dead pattern returned 105
# where the output stage says 0, while round 168's --kmap baseline returned 0.
# The difference between them is the input, not the weights: 105 is the input
# leaking through the B record. B is the weight zero point correction in the
# domain the weight is stored in, and a nibble is NOT biased by 0x80 the way a
# byte weight is, so int4's B is 0 and this tree was writing 0x80. With the input
# 32 above its zero point over 64 taps, 0x80 * 2048 * mult is 210, which is twice
# what was seen. Corrected.
#
# ROUND 169's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   ENTRY 3. CHARSIU_W4 runs the same dense matmul, impulse and bias ramp that
#   int8 passes byte exact, with int4 weights confined to a nibble and the
#   reference reading the same two's complement nibble.
#
#     byte exact, or within a count
#               int4 computes. Halving the weight bytes halves a token, so this
#               is the 2x the bandwidth measurement said was the only one left.
#     the impulse is right and the dense one is not
#               the layout is right and something in the accumulation across
#               nibbles is not, which is a much smaller place to look.
#     both wrong but the impulse structurally right
#               the layout holds and the arithmetic does not: B, the nibble sign
#               convention, or 0x1030's one-times branch, in that order.
#     nothing resembling the reference
#               the layout reading from two rounds of patterns does not survive
#               contact with real values, and the packer goes back to the board
#               with position encoded weights rather than live-or-dead ones.
#
#   ENTRY 2 IS THE CONTROL. The packer, the group helpers and the B record all
#   changed this round and int8 goes through every one of them. If int8 is not
#   still byte exact, nothing int4 does can be read.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What earlier rounds settled, kept because the rules below were written against
# it.
#
# 2026-08-15 round 168. A byte's two nibbles are the SAME output channel at two
# different k. Which two k is what is left, and an input impulse reads it.
#
# ROUND 167's RESULT. The control passed first: every nibble dead came back flat
# 105 and every nibble live came back 127, so int4 weights with int8 activations
# do compute, which the vendor never does and which nothing guaranteed.
#
# Then low nibbles live and high nibbles live gave the IDENTICAL output, a single
# value across all 64 channels in both. That rules out every N pairing there is.
# n with n+1 would have made them complementary on even and odd channels; n with
# n+32 would have split them at the group boundary and the summary line says
# "distinct 1" for the whole vector, not just the printed half. So both nibbles
# of a byte feed the SAME output channel, and the group of 64 that charsiu
# carries from the RK3588 notes is not what this silicon does.
#
# The "only the first byte live" line is NOT a second confirmation, though it
# reads like one. It lit channel 0 alone, which both hypotheses predict, because
# only the low nibble was set. What it does say is that the origin is at n = 0.
#
# WHAT IS WRONG AND WORTH SAYING. The absolute values are not right. Every nibble
# dead returned 105 where the output stage says 0, and the low nibble delta was
# 14 where the tap count says about 6. The layout signal is a DIFFERENCE between
# patterns so it survives that, but int4 is not computing correctly, and the
# coefficient buffer, the nibble's own zero point convention and 0x1030's
# one-times branch are all still unchecked.
#
# ROUND 168's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run. A byte
# holds two k for one channel, but which two: k and k+1, adjacent, or k and k+32,
# the two halves of a group? Low against high cannot see it, because both put the
# pair in the same channel.
#
#   ENTRY 3, --kmap. Hold the weights at low nibbles live and high dead, and make
#   the INPUT an impulse at one k with everything else at the zero point. Then
#   the output moves off its baseline exactly when that k sits in a low nibble,
#   and walking k reads the map directly. k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 15, 16, 30, 31, 32, 33,
#   62, 63 is enough to separate the two.
#
#     a delta on EVEN k only
#               a byte holds k and k+1, so the packer interleaves
#     a delta on k %% 64 < 32
#               a byte holds k and k+32, so the packer splits a group in half
#     a delta on every k
#               the weights are not being read the way this assumes, and round
#               167's low against high was reading something else. That would
#               retract the pairing result too, so it is the outcome to hope
#               against rather than the one to explain away.
#     no delta anywhere
#               the input impulse is not reaching the MAC, which is about the
#               input packing and not about int4 at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What earlier rounds settled, kept because the rules below were written against
# it.
#
# 2026-08-15 round 167. int4, because it is the only 2x left. The registers for
# it are confirmed offline; the LAYOUT is what the board has to say.
#
# ROUND 166 DID NOT BREAK THE READING. Seven shapes at 32 chained tasks, and the
# two pairs put in to break it held:
#
#   1.05 MB as K=1024 N=1024 and as K=2048 N=512   111.56 and 111.24 us, 0.3% apart
#   2.10 MB as K=2048 N=1024 and as K=1024 N=2048  208.06 and 211.21 us, 1.5% apart
#
# Fitting all seven together gives us per task = 26.3 + weight_MB * 84.3, which is
# 11.9 GB/s plus 26 us, every point inside 10% of the line and most inside 4%. A
# further 172 us of fixed cost sits on each submit and is what chaining removes.
# The CPU denominator works now and reads 5078 us against the NPU's 201.
#
# So decode is DRAM bound. Llama-3.2-1B reads about 973 M projection weights per
# token, once each: int8 is 85 ms and 11.8 tokens a second, int4 is 44 ms and
# 22.7, against the vendor's shipped 13. int4 is the only 2x on the table.
#
# WHAT IS ALREADY SETTLED ABOUT int4, offline, from the vendor's own file. The
# .rkllm carries 3328 four bit streams and 40 eight bit ones, so the precisions
# can be diffed against each other with no board at all
# (vendor-capture/int4_regs.py):
#
#   0x100c   0x00000000 int8, 0x00600120 int4, plus 0x20000000 for 16 bit
#            activations
#   0x101c   real bytes, k * n / 2
#   0x1020   k / 2, and 0x1030's high half the same, where int8 carries twice
#   surf     follows the ACTIVATION precision and not the weight precision
#
# All of those were already right in charsiu EXCEPT 0x100c, which its job emitter
# wrote as 0 for int4 as well as int8, so an int4 job would have asked for an
# int8 weight fetch and any layout read from it would have been noise. Its other
# emitter had the right constant. Fixed this round.
#
# ROUND 167's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run. What no
# file contains is the LAYOUT: a .rkllm gives geometry, not the order the nibbles
# sit in, and the group of 64 in charsiu is copied from the RK3588 notes and has
# never been confirmed here. The question is which two weights share a byte:
#
#   K PAIRING   [n/32][k/64][n%32][k%64], the low nibble is an even k
#   N PAIRING   [n/64][k/32][n%64][k%32], the low nibble is an even n
#
#   ENTRY 3 fills every low nibble live and every high nibble dead, and the two
#   answer differently in a way no scale or requant can disguise, because the
#   signal is WHICH CHANNELS ARE NONZERO rather than what value they hold.
#
#     every channel nonzero and roughly equal
#               nibbles pair along K. Half of each channel's taps are live.
#     even and odd channels split, half live and half dead
#               nibbles pair along N.
#     something else
#               neither hypothesis, and the first byte entry says where the
#               origin is before guessing again.
#
#   THE FIRST TWO LINES ARE THE CONTROL and must be read first. Every nibble
#   dead must come back flat; every nibble live must not. The vendor only ever
#   runs int4 weights with 16 bit activations and charsiu has never run a 16 bit
#   activation at all, so this asks for int4 weights with the int8 activations
#   that are known to work.
#
#     dead and live look the same
#               that combination does not compute, no layout can be read from
#               the rest of the entry, and the next round is fp16 activations
#               rather than another layout guess.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What earlier rounds settled, kept because the rules below were written against
# it.
#
# 2026-08-15 round 166. The cost is the WEIGHT FETCH, not dispatch and not MAC.
# This round tries to break that reading rather than build on it.
#
# ROUND 165's NUMBERS, on this board. Sweeping chained tasks from 1 to 128 at
# three shapes, the marginal cost of one more task is
#
#   M=1  K=2048 N=1024   2.10 MB of weights   201.9 us   10.4 GB/s
#   M=1  K=512  N=1024   0.52 MB               57.4 us    9.1 GB/s
#   M=32 K=2048 N=1024   2.10 MB              217.6 us    9.6 GB/s
#
# M IS NEARLY FREE. The same weights at M = 32 cost 1.08 times what they cost at
# M = 1 while doing 32 times the arithmetic. So the cost is not the MAC and not
# the row count, and it tracks the weight bytes at close to 10 GB/s, which is
# about half of what this board's LPDDR5 delivers.
#
# There is a fixed cost of 180 to 195 us per submit on top, which chaining
# removes: a 0.5 MB projection gains 4.4 times from batching and a 2 MB one only
# 1.9 times, because the fixed part is a bigger share of the small one.
#
# Two jobs of eight were consistently about 5 percent WORSE than one job of
# sixteen, at all three shapes. That is what a DRAM bound workload does: a
# second core cannot add bandwidth.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS ENOUGH TO ATTACK. Llama-3.2-1B reads about 973 M projection
# weights per token, once each. At 10 GB/s that is 97 ms a token in int8 and
# 49 ms in int4, against the vendor's shipped 13 tokens a second. If this reading
# holds, decode is DRAM bound, int4 is the only 2x available, and the plan
# changes; so it should be attacked before anything is built on it.
#
# ROUND 166's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run.
#
#   ENTRY 3 is the falsifier. charsiu_bench --shapes runs seven shapes at 32
#   chained tasks and prints the implied GB/s for each, and TWO OF THE PAIRS HAVE
#   THE SAME WEIGHT BYTES IN DIFFERENT SHAPES:
#
#     K=2048 N=1024 and K=1024 N=2048   both 2.10 MB
#     K=1024 N=1024 and K=2048 N=512    both 1.05 MB
#
#     both pairs match and the GB/s column is flat within about 15 percent
#               the cost IS the weight fetch. Decode is DRAM bound, int4 is the
#               lever, and nothing about dispatch is worth optimising.
#     a pair comes apart
#               there is a shape term the bytes reading does not have, the
#               token rate arithmetic above comes down, and the next round is
#               about what that term is.
#
#   ENTRY 4 repeats the task sweep at the projection shape and prints the
#   marginal and fixed costs separately, computed from 128 tasks against 64 so
#   there is no submit cost in the difference at all.
#
#   THE CPU DENOMINATOR was broken in round 165: it printed 0.0 us because the
#   compiler deleted the loop, whose result went into a local compared against a
#   value it could never take. A denominator that reads zero is worse than none,
#   since the ratio it feeds is the whole RK3588 argument. It is volatile now and
#   its sink is printed.
#
#   ENTRY 2 IS THE CONTROL, and it also caught something. The BYTE EXACT counter
#   added last round immediately showed 512 to 1024 is 1023 of 1024 exact with
#   one channel off by one, not the 1024 the README claimed. The 64 wide probes
#   are all 64 of 64. Entry 2 must reproduce both, or the buffer lifetime change
#   in this round moved something.
#
