#
# 2026-08-15 round 163. THERE IS NO FUSED RELU. The output is a signed byte,
# the stage is solved, and the blocker this project has carried since round 158
# was an artefact of reading those bytes as unsigned.
#
# ROUND 162'S RESULT. Its control did its job first: the pre-162 raw input
# reproduced round 161's wrong numbers to the byte, so the new binary ran and the
# input bias is what changed. With the input biased, the negative MAC probe came
# back 128 for the first 32 channels and then EXACTLY the cpu row, 130 132 134
# through to 191, irregular step at index 43 included. The bias ramp's residual,
# the "about three counts" that six rounds wrote off as rounding, is gone.
#
# Then the fold entry gave the thing away. Folding the zero point into A and
# leaving the offset at 0 produced the cpu row exactly for 32 channels and then
# a flat 127, and 127 is not a number this hardware should have any reason for.
#
# Fitting all three entries at once, with one model and no exceptions:
#
#     out = clamp(requant + offset, -128, 127), stored as int8
#
# 64 of 64 bytes on each of the three, none off by a single count. The entries
# differ in the input bias, the offset and the fold, so nothing in that model is
# free.
#
# What it means, and it corrects two things this tree has said for ten rounds.
#
# There is no fused ReLU. The floor at "the output zero point" is the -128 rail
# of a signed byte, and what parked every negative result on that rail was
# writing -128 into 0x40ac. Rounds 152 to 158 wrote it because they were reading
# signed bytes as unsigned and needed the 128 back, so the fix and the symptom
# were the same line.
#
# And 0x40ac adds with the sign its name implies. Round 155 recorded the opposite
# for the same reason.
#
# ROUND 163's QUESTION, and its decision rule, written before the run. The
# output is int8, so the reference is int8 and the output zero point is 0, which
# is what a projection wants anyway. Every prediction below is arithmetic.
#
#   ENTRY 2. The negative MAC, output zero point 0, offset 0, reference signed.
#   requant = 2.048 * (c - 32) runs -66 to +63, inside a signed byte at both
#   ends, so nothing clamps.
#
#     all 64 channels exact against the cpu
#               charsiu computes a SIGNED matmul end to end. That is the whole
#               of what it was blocked on, and the runtime is next.
#     the negative half still on a rail
#               the model is wrong somewhere and entry 3 says whether the
#               binary even changed.
#
#   ENTRY 3, THE CONTROL. CHARSIU_UINT8_OUT with CHARSIU_OUT_OFF=-128, which is
#   the configuration every round up to 162 used. It MUST reproduce round 162's
#   entry 2 exactly: 128 for the first 32 channels, then 130 132 134 up to 191.
#   If the old configuration no longer produces the old answer, nothing in this
#   round can be read.
#
#   ENTRY 4. The bias ramp with the same signed settings. requant runs -48 to
#   +53, so again nothing clamps.
#
#     all 64 exact
#               the output stage is finished for both paths into it.
#
#   ENTRY 5. A dense matmul and an impulse, signed. These are the first runs in
#   this project where the reference and the hardware are supposed to agree on
#   every channel with nothing clamped anywhere.
